<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">East Jersey</h1><p class="st-meta">1674 CE – 1702 CE · capital Perth Amboy</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern half of colonial New Jersey, owned by Carteret's heirs and later by a board of Scottish and Quaker proprietors, with its capital at Perth Amboy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-berkeley-1st-baron-berkeley-of-stratton">Lord Berkeley</a> sold his share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> in 1674, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-carteret">Sir George Carteret</a> kept the eastern part, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintipartite-deed">Quintipartite Deed</a> of 1676 fixed the line dividing East <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Jersey</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-jersey">West Jersey</a>. After Carteret's death his heirs sold the colony in 1682 to 24 proprietors, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and Scots, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-barclay">Robert Barclay</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-carteret">Philip Carteret</a>, a cousin of the founder, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-new-jersey">Elizabethtown</a> through the 1670s, and frequently clashed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-new-york">Province of New York</a> over customs and authority. The Scottish proprietors founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth-amboy">Perth Amboy</a> as their capital, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-barclay">Barclay</a> served as absentee governor from 1682 to 1688. Settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> farmed and resisted quitrents. In 1688 the colony was added to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-new-england">Dominion of New England</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Disorder over land titles led the proprietors to surrender government to the Crown in 1702, when East and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-jersey">West Jersey</a> were reunited as the royal <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-new-jersey">Province of New Jersey</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/board-of-proprietors-of-east-jersey">Board of Proprietors of East Jersey</a> kept its land rights for centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/east-jersey.jpg" alt="East Jersey" width="320" height="196"><figcaption>Image: Martocticvs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:English_Red_Ensign_1620.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1674 CE – 1702 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Perth Amboy</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philip Carteret, Robert Barclay</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Province of East Jersey</dd></dl></aside></div>
